  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Monday...\n\nA persistent pattern will prevail through the end of the work \nweek, with upper level troughing to our southwest and high \npressure to the east. This will put the region under broad\ndiffluent south to southwest flow aloft, though high pressure \nnosing into eastern MT will keep higher heights in place. This \nwill bring high temperatures in the 80s to 90s most days, \ngenerally warmest over the east. Southeast to east flow will keep \nbreezy conditions in place east of Billings, where daily gusts \nbetween 20-40 mph are possible. Widespread fire weather concerns \nare not expected at this time due to green up.\n\nWhile above normal PWATs are expected (0.75 to 1 inch most days),\nforcing will be mainly west of the area. Isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms (15-40% chance) are possible most days, \ngreatest over the higher terrain and western areas. Strong to\nsevere thunderstorms are possible at times, bringing gusty winds\nand moderate rainfall. There is currently a marginal risk for\nsevere weather over our far west today, with wind being the main\nthreat.\n\nHeading into the weekend and early next week, ensembles have\nremained in agreement in an area of low pressure lifting from\nCalifornia north and east through the Great Basin into the\nNorthern Rockies. Though there remains uncertainty, this pattern\nlooks to bring a cool down (temperatures down into the 70s) with\nincreasing shower and thunderstorm chances into early next week.\nSTP",
